On Wed, 27 Jul 2005, ivica wrote:

> Hi all,
>
> anyone has an idea what would one do if root pass was lost (any version of
> tru64?)
> Any link...?

If you can get the system to a SRM prompt (ie, the three angle arrows >>>)
then you can type 'boot -fl s' to boot the system to single user mode.
Then you can edit out the password field for root's entry in /etc/passwd -
this is the second field, after the first colon separator, eg:

         root:IpGjn1Mxmlf8.:0:1:system PRIVILEGED account:/:/bin/sh
              ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
              |
         (delete this)

so that root's entry in this file now looks like:

         root::0:1:system PRIVILEGED account:/:/bin/sh

and then reboot with ctrl-D or whatever.

To edit /etc/passwd, you can use ed (which resides in /bin) but if you're
not happy with using this editor, you may have to mount at least /usr/bin
to be able to use vi or emacs - a quick way of doing this is to type
'bcheckrc'. vi may complain about 'unknown terminal type' and present you
with a line editor display (ie, not full screen) - try the command 'env
TERM=vt100 vi /etc/passwd' instead.

Hope this helps - and don't forget to set a new password for root!

Andy





