On Wed, 27 Jul 2005 14:04:53 +0200, ivica wrote:

> Hi all,
> 
> anyone has an idea what would one do if root pass was lost (any version of 
> tru64?)
> Any link...?
> 
If the system doens't have a bios password set then the system can be
hacked by booting to single user mode and setting a new password.
To do this power off the system and then during the boot process use ^c or
^p to interupt the boot before it begins, then at the >>> prompt pass the
's' argument to the kernel.
i.e.>>> boot -fl s
This will bring you to a root shell on a system running in single user
mode. The system filesystems will be mounted read-only or not mounted at
all, so use the command bcheckrc to mount them all read write.
Then you can use the passwd command to set any root password you need.
Adam
PS If you have trouble interupting the boot process (The timing can be
tricky), then you can always remove the power to the boot disk and then
boot. When the system fails to boot you can change the console boot mode to
halt and gain access to the >>> prompt that way (Ask for more detail if you
need it).


